# Rank 1722 / CVE-2023-46729 (GHSA-2rmr-xw8m-22q9, CWE-918): @sentry/nextjs's
# optional `tunnelRoute` works by injecting a rewrite rule into the user's
# Next.js config, so that `GET <tunnelPath>?o=<orgid>&p=<projectid>` is
# forwarded to `https://o:orgid.ingest.sentry.io/api/:projectid/envelope/`
# and the browser's ad-blocker never sees an ingest hostname. The rule is
# declarative: setUpTunnelRewriteRules in packages/nextjs/src/config/
# withSentryConfig.ts sets `userNextConfig.rewrites` to a function returning
# `{ source, has: [{type:'query', key:'o', value:'(?<orgid>.*)'}, {type:'query',
# key:'p', value:'(?<projectid>.*)'}], destination: 'https://...' }`. Next.js
# turns `has` into route-match regexes and interpolates the named captures
# into `destination` as `:name` -- and because the captures are `.*`, they
# accept every character, so the orgid the requester typed becomes the
# authority and the projectid the path of the URL the *server* fetches, and
# the response comes back to the requester. The tunnel is an open proxy:
# internal addresses, port scans, cloud metadata. The advisory's severity is
# Medium (CVSS 6.1) and its workaround is to drop `tunnelRoute` entirely.
#
# The fixed revision (ddbda3c0, "fix(nextjs): Strictly validate tunnel target
# parameters") changes two regex bodies and nothing else:
#   (?<orgid>.*)       -> (?<orgid>[a-fA-F0-9]*)
#   (?<projectid>.*)   -> (?<projectid>\d*)
# which are the documented alphabets of a Sentry organisation id (hex) and
# project id (decimal). The remediation is present at the fixed revision and the
# unrestricted form at its parent, so the pair discriminates cleanly.
#
# Detection is a JavaScript function-scope binding,
# `rewriteTargetFromUnrestrictedCapture`, emitting the new
# `code.RewriteTargetFromUnrestrictedCapture` (VYQL-RF-049, CWE-918/CWE-20).
# It reads three literal facts about a rewrite-rule object and judges none:
# a matcher type from the router's documented set (query, host, header,
# cookie), a capture that accepts any character (`>.*`), and a destination
# carrying a scheme. The object-literal *keys* (`source`, `has`,
# `destination`) emit no tokens in this frontend, so the binding anchors on
# the documented matcher-type vocabulary instead -- which is what makes it
# rule-agnostic: it never names `rewrites`, `tunnelRoute`, Sentry, or any
# identifier from the fixing commit. A capture restricted by any alphabet,
# anchor or bound removes the any-character literal and stops the report, so
# the discriminator is the capture's strength and not the spelling the fixed revision
# happened to choose; a destination with no scheme stays inside the origin
# and is not reported at all.
#
# Concepts considered before minting: `code.WebhookUrlFetchMissingSsrfGuard`
# describes a delivery fetch of a caller-configured callback URL, and this is
# a declarative route table, not a fetch call. `code.RequestHostUrlValidation
# Bypass` and `code.WeakRedirectUrlValidation` describe validation helpers
# that accept too much; here no validation code exists, the matcher itself is
# the admission control. `code.ConfigUrlStoredBeforeSchemeValidation` is a
# persisted connector setting, not a per-request capture. `code.Unanchored
# HostPathRoutingMatch` is the nearest neighbour -- a router selecting a
# backend too loosely -- but its target is *selected* by a substring test
# while here the target is *assembled* from the captured bytes, and its
# review category is authorization. The class this rank adds is new: the
# admission control and the outbound target are both static literals in one
# function, which is exactly what the earlier members of the URL-allowlist
# family lacked -- at those ranks the guard is a runtime check whose
# strength no binding could read, and the honest outcome is no coverage.
#
# Measured on the whole checkout of both revisions (2142 JavaScript files,
# `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB`, three runs each):
# 601 findings before the binding, none touching withSentryConfig.ts; with
# it, 603 at the vulnerable revision 4371b2c8 and 601 at the fixed revision ddbda3c0,
# identical across all three runs on each side, the fingerprint delta being
# two VYQL-RF-049 rows on packages/nextjs/src/config/withSentryConfig.ts at
# the two enclosing functions (setUpTunnelRewriteRules and the rewrites
# callback it installs).
#
# Collateral, enumerated over every corpus checkout rather than sampled:
# grepping all clones for the binding's three literals co-occurring in one
# file yields 44 files, which deduplicate to eight distinct artifacts -- the
# yarn 4.0.2/4.6.0/4.12.0 bundles, the swagger-ui bundle, the swagger-ui
# es-bundle, Next.js's compiled cssnano, the nocodb dist CLI and MediaWiki's
# Uri.js, plus Next.js's own custom-routes fixtures and this rank's repros.
# Scanning one of each: zero new rows on every bundle and on Uri.js, and 4
# rows on the Next.js fixtures -- one of them the same weakness (`type:
# 'host'`, `(?<subdomain>.*)-test.example.com`, destination
# `https://:subdomain.example.com/...`), the other three the loose
# function-scope co-occurrence of a `.*` header capture from one rule with
# another rule's absolute destination in the same config function. The
# swagger-ui es-bundle times out at 180s with or without the binding, so it
# measures nothing either way. Two whole trees scanned clean: qwik (1161
# findings, 0 new) and RSSHub (1919 findings, 0 new).

test "rank1722: the tunnel rewrite captures the org and project ids with .* into an absolute destination"
  lang javascript
  file packages/nextjs/src/config/withSentryConfig.ts
  expect VYQL-RF-049
  expect_review code.RewriteTargetFromUnrestrictedCapture
  code
  ```
  interface NextConfigObject {
    [key: string]: any;
    rewrites?: any;
  }

  function setUpTunnelRewriteRules(userNextConfig: NextConfigObject, tunnelPath: string): void {
    const originalRewrites = userNextConfig.rewrites;

    userNextConfig.rewrites = async (...args: unknown[]) => {
      const injectedRewrite = {
        // Matched rewrite routes look like `[tunnelPath]?o=[orgid]&p=[projectid]`
        source: `${tunnelPath}(/?)`,
        has: [
          {
            type: 'query',
            key: 'o',
            value: '(?<orgid>.*)',
          },
          {
            type: 'query',
            key: 'p',
            value: '(?<projectid>.*)',
          },
        ],
        destination: 'https://o:orgid.ingest.sentry.io/api/:projectid/envelope/?hsts=0',
      };

      if (typeof originalRewrites !== 'function') {
        return [injectedRewrite];
      }

      const originalRewritesResult = await originalRewrites(...args);
      if (Array.isArray(originalRewritesResult)) {
        return [injectedRewrite, ...originalRewritesResult];
      }
      return originalRewritesResult;
    };
  }
  ```

test "rank1722: the tunnel rewrite restricts each capture to its identifier's alphabet"
  lang javascript
  file packages/nextjs/src/config/withSentryConfig.ts
  reject VYQL-RF-049
  reject_review code.RewriteTargetFromUnrestrictedCapture
  code
  ```
  interface NextConfigObject {
    [key: string]: any;
    rewrites?: any;
  }

  function setUpTunnelRewriteRules(userNextConfig: NextConfigObject, tunnelPath: string): void {
    const originalRewrites = userNextConfig.rewrites;

    userNextConfig.rewrites = async (...args: unknown[]) => {
      const injectedRewrite = {
        source: `${tunnelPath}(/?)`,
        has: [
          {
            type: 'query',
            key: 'o',
            value: '(?<orgid>[a-fA-F0-9]*)',
          },
          {
            type: 'query',
            key: 'p',
            value: '(?<projectid>\\d*)',
          },
        ],
        destination: 'https://o:orgid.ingest.sentry.io/api/:projectid/envelope/?hsts=0',
      };

      if (typeof originalRewrites !== 'function') {
        return [injectedRewrite];
      }

      const originalRewritesResult = await originalRewrites(...args);
      if (Array.isArray(originalRewritesResult)) {
        return [injectedRewrite, ...originalRewritesResult];
      }
      return originalRewritesResult;
    };
  }
  ```

test "rank1722: the same weakness in an ordinary next.config rewrites block, on the host matcher"
  lang javascript
  file next.config.js
  expect VYQL-RF-049
  expect_review code.RewriteTargetFromUnrestrictedCapture
  code
  ```
  // A tenant-facing rewrite: any subdomain of the platform is forwarded to
  // that tenant's own origin, with the subdomain taken from the request.
  module.exports = {
    async rewrites() {
      return [
        {
          source: '/api/v1/:path*',
          has: [
            {
              type: 'host',
              value: '(?<tenant>.*).app.example.com',
            },
          ],
          destination: 'https://:tenant.tenant-origin.internal/api/v1/:path*',
        },
      ];
    },
  };
  ```

test "rank1722: an anchored, alphabet-restricted capture is a guarded rewrite"
  lang javascript
  file next.config.js
  reject VYQL-RF-049
  reject_review code.RewriteTargetFromUnrestrictedCapture
  code
  ```
  // The same tenant rewrite with the capture restricted to the identifier's
  // alphabet and anchored: the requester can no longer move the host.
  module.exports = {
    async rewrites() {
      return [
        {
          source: '/api/v1/:path*',
          has: [
            {
              type: 'host',
              key: undefined,
              value: '^(?<tenant>[a-z0-9-]{1,63})\\.app\\.example\\.com$',
            },
          ],
          destination: 'https://:tenant.tenant-origin.internal/api/v1/:path*',
        },
      ];
    },
  };
  ```

test "rank1722: a capture restricted to a non-hex alphabet is guarded whichever alphabet it picks"
  lang javascript
  file next.config.js
  reject VYQL-RF-049
  reject_review code.RewriteTargetFromUnrestrictedCapture
  code
  ```
  module.exports = {
    async rewrites() {
      return [
        {
          source: '/strings.json',
          has: [
            {
              type: 'query',
              key: 'lang',
              value: '(?<locale>[a-z]{2}(-[A-Z]{2})?)',
            },
          ],
          destination: 'https://cdn.example.com/i18n/:locale/strings.json',
        },
      ];
    },
  };
  ```

test "rank1722: an origin-internal rewrite with an unrestricted capture moves nothing off the host"
  lang javascript
  file next.config.js
  reject VYQL-RF-049
  reject_review code.RewriteTargetFromUnrestrictedCapture
  code
  ```
  // The capture is unrestricted, but the destination carries no scheme: the
  // request is re-routed inside the same origin and never leaves it.
  module.exports = {
    async rewrites() {
      return [
        {
          source: '/legacy/:section*',
          has: [
            {
              type: 'query',
              key: 'view',
              value: '(?<view>.*)',
            },
          ],
          destination: '/legacy/:view/:section*',
        },
      ];
    },
  };
  ```

